{
  "comment": "How every wire of the worker protocol reads and writes JSON and base64, which Prima does with Jason and Elixir's Base on both sides of each wire (Prima.WorkerWire, Prima.WorkerAuth, Prima.Assignment, Prima.HostAPI, Prima.LocusPool, Prima.RunnerRelay, Prima.RunnerControl; none with the standard library's JSON), so a message means to the side that acts on it what it meant to the side that signed it. reads are documents (doc_b64, the bytes) each read as value: a member given twice is its first, an integer of any size is an integer kept whole, -0 is the integer 0, a fraction or an exponent is no integer, and a number below a double's range reads as zero. refused are documents no reader takes: bytes that are no UTF-8 or that spell a surrogate, an escape naming half a surrogate pair, a raw control character in a string, whitespace other than space, tab, line feed and carriage return, a number past a double's range, an integer of more than 1,024 characters, and anything after the one value. writes are values each written as bytes_b64: only quote, backslash and the control characters escaped, backspace, tab, line feed, form feed and carriage return by their two-character escapes and every other control character as \\u00XX in uppercase hexadecimal, so solidus, <, >, &, U+2028, U+2029 and DEL are written as themselves, and a plain map's members in the order of their names' bytes. base64url are texts as Base.url_decode64/2 with padding false reads them (bytes_hex, or null for a text it refuses): unpadded or with exactly the padding it would carry, bits past the last byte ignored, nothing outside the URL alphabet and no line break; base64 are texts as Base.decode64/1 reads them, its padding required. apps/prima/test/prima/wire_json_test.exs holds Jason and Base to every case, and the Go readers of the worker protocol (apps/locus/keeper/internal/wire/wirejson and macenvelope) are held to the same.",
